<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baltic Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baltic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baltic Sea is an arm of the Atlantic in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Baltic states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Control of its trade and coasts was the central aim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> in the seventeenth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> exported grain through its ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> access to the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baltic-sea.jpg" alt="Baltic Sea" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Ziegler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Baltic_Sea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Baltic Sea is an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> stretching from 53°N to 66°N latitude and from 10°E to 30°E longitude. It is about 1,600 km long, an average of 193 km wide and an average of 55 metres deep, with a maximum depth of 459 metres on the Swedish side of the centre. It has about 8,000 km of coastline and occupies a basin formed by glacial erosion during the last few ice ages. It drains through the Danish Straits into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, and includes the Gulf of Bothnia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> and the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>.</p><p>Before the Pleistocene a wide plain lay here around a great river that palaeontologists call the Eridanos, and several glacial episodes scooped out its bed into the sea basin. The later stages are named and dated, from the Baltic Ice Lake (16,000–11,700 years before the present) through the Yoldia Sea, the Ancylus Lake and the Littorina Sea to the Post-Littorina Sea of the last 4,000 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the sea was known as the Mare Suebicum or Mare Sarmaticum. Tacitus, writing in 98 CE, described the Mare Suebicum, named for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, as a brackish sea in which ice broke apart in spring. The first to name it the Baltic Sea (Mare Balticum) was the eleventh-century German chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a>, who compared it to a belt stretching through the land; the precise origin of the name is unknown.</p><p>In the early Middle Ages Norse merchants built a trade empire all around the Baltic, later fighting the Wendish tribes of the southern shore for control of it. They also used the rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as trade routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. This is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, and the Scandinavians have called the Baltic Austmarr, the Eastern Sea, since then. Historically the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> collected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sound-toll">Sound Dues</a> from ships at the entrances to the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Baltic is enclosed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and is the world's largest brackish water basin. Artificial waterways join it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal">White Sea–Baltic Canal</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> by the Kiel Canal. Its fauna mixes marine and freshwater species, and the common blue mussel makes up more than 90 per cent of its animal biomass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baltic Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Klaipėda Region gave the country its only access to the <b>Baltic Sea</b> and was an important industrial centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>British and French ships began bombarding the Russian fortress of Sveaborg from beyond the range of its artillery, in the Åland War, the <b>Baltic Sea</b> theatre of the Crimean War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-9">9 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sweden returned Novgorod, which it had occupied, but kept Ingria and Kexholm, cutting Russia off from the <b>Baltic Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
